.TH SCHEDCMP 1
.SH NAME
schedcmp \- compare recorded Dis schedules
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B schedcmp
[
.B -v
]
.I file1 file2
.RI [ file3 ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Schedcmp
compares schedules recorded by
.B INFERNO_SCHED_RECORD
(see
.IR emu (1)),
and answers the question that decides whether forcing a schedule on a later
run would be worth anything: how much do two runs of the same session actually
differ?
.PP
That is not one number, so it reports several \(em all events, then with
.B iyield
removed, then quanta alone, then the points where the virtual machine changed
hands, then the order of processes irrespective of what each did.
.PP
Removing
.B iyield
matters more than anything else.
A yield is a
.B vmachine
kproc handing the slot to a process already waiting for it: host-thread
bookkeeping that changes nothing about which Dis program runs when.
Left in, it swamps the comparison.
.PP
.B -v
also prints each schedule's event counts.
.SH EXAMPLE
Three runs of the same sequential shell session:
.IP
.EX
all events                lengths 588 589 591   identical=no
without iyield            lengths 420 420 420   identical=YES
quanta only               lengths  74  74  74   identical=YES
host-call boundaries      lengths 346 346 346   identical=YES
pid order, without iyield lengths 420 420 420   identical=YES
.EE
.PP
So Dis execution here is already exactly reproducible, and the only thing
that varied was the yields.
.SH "WHAT WAS MEASURED"
Three regimes, and they differ:
.IP
.EX
session                       runs  non-yield events  order identical
sequential shell                14    352, all equal  yes, 14 of 14
wm, input replayed               3  2776, 2776, 2792  no, diverges at 83
refstress, 16 processes          3     50592 - 67440  no, diverges at 145
.EE
.PP
The first row is the surprise.
Fourteen runs of the same shell session produced byte-identical schedules once
.B iyield
was removed \(em same quanta, same handoffs, same processes in the same order.
Dis execution there is reproducible without anything being forced.
.PP
Not guaranteed, though: one divergent pair was seen in about twenty runs of
that same session, with equal lengths and a different order.
So it is reliably reproducible rather than certainly so, and a claim of
determinism would be too strong.
.PP
Every session with a window in it diverged, and quickly.
.PP
It matters how a session ends.
These runs end by running
.IR quitall (1)
from the recorded input, so both stop for the same reason.
An earlier version of this measurement stopped them with a timer from outside
and reported that two
.IR wm (1)
runs did an identical amount of work; they had merely been cut off at similar
points, and once they were allowed to finish, two of three runs matched and
the third did not.
.PP
So: forcing a recorded schedule would buy nothing in a sequential session,
which needs no help.
It is the only route for a session with a window in it \(em and even there the
amount of work is not fixed between runs, so a forced order will sometimes be
impossible to follow, because the process the recording says to run next is
blocked and the one that would wake it is the one being held back.
.SS "Whether the recorder moves what it measures"
Every number above was taken with recording on, and recording takes a lock and
a wakeup on the scheduler's hot path.
If that serialised things, a schedule would look more reproducible than the
virtual machine really is, and the first row would be an artefact of the
instrument.
.PP
Schedules cannot be compared with the recorder off, so the check has to be
indirect.
.IR Refstress (1)
reports how many processes are inside a region containing a host call, which is
order-sensitive and needs no recording, over twenty runs each way:
.IP
.EX
recorder off   min 3  max 16  median 16  mean 13.3
recorder on    min 3  max 16  median 16  mean 11.7
.EE
.PP
Same range, same median, and the difference in mean is small against a
distribution that is mostly 16 with a tail at 3 and 4.
The property
.IR refstress (1)
asserts \(em that exactly one process executes Dis at a time \(em held in all
forty runs either way.
.PP
So the recorder does not remove the concurrency it is there to observe.
That is not proof that it perturbs nothing, and it is worth redoing if these
numbers are ever load-bearing for something bigger than a decision about what
to build next.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/schedcmp.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR emu (1),
.IR refstress (1),
.IR inputtest (1)
.SH BUGS
It compares schedules, which say which process ran and in what order, not what
any of them computed.
.PP
Comparing across
.B -c0
and
.B -c1
is refused rather than reported, because the interpreter and the compiler
reach system calls at different points and the comparison would measure only
that.
.PP
A schedule that overflowed its ring says so when recorded, but nothing here
re-checks it.
